<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derry</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Londonderry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derry, officially Londonderry, is a city on the River Foyle in the north-west of Northern Ireland. It grew from a sixth-century monastery associated with Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> and was rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a> companies during the Plantation of Ulster, its walls being completed in 1619. Its Protestant defenders withstood a siege by the army of James II for 105 days in 1689.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/derry.jpg" alt="Derry" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aaronwarduk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foyle_Bridge_Derry_at_Dusk_Oblique.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Derry lies on both banks of the River Foyle, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a>, with which it has had a close link for many centuries. The original walled city stands on a hill on the west bank, where the river once branched and enclosed the hill as an island. Over the centuries the western branch dried up and became the low-lying district now called the Bogside. The name is an anglicisation of the Irish Daire or Doire, meaning oak-grove, and derives from the earliest reference, Daire Calgaich, the oak-grove of Calgach.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Saint Colmcille, a holy man from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">Tír Chonaill</a>, the old name for almost all of modern County Donegal, is traditionally seen as the founder of the original settlement. Until 1610 the west bank belonged to Donegal. In that year the Crown transferred it to The Honourable The Irish Society and combined it with County Coleraine, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a> and a large part of County Tyrone to form County Londonderry. Planters organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> livery companies rebuilt the town with high walls against Irish insurgents who opposed the plantation, and in 1613 it was renamed Londonderry.</p><p>In 1649 the garrison, which supported the Parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, was besieged by Scottish Presbyterian forces loyal to Charles I. It was relieved by an unlikely alliance of Roundhead troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-monck">George Monck</a> and the Irish Catholic general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owen-roe-oneill">Owen Roe O'Neill</a>. The city was rebuilt in the 18th century, many Georgian houses survive, and its first bridge over the Foyle was built in 1790. In the 18th and 19th centuries the port was an important point of embarkation for Irish emigrants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and shirt-making, begun in 1831, became the main industry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Derry is a cathedral city of 85,279 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, the second largest in Northern Ireland after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>. It is the only city in Ireland whose walls survive completely intact, and they are the largest monument in state care in Northern Ireland. Its landmarks include St Columb's Cathedral, the first post-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> cathedral built for an Anglican church. In the late 1960s the city became a focal point of the civil rights movement in Northern Ireland, and the Troubles are widely regarded as having begun there with the Battle of the Bogside.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Derry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Williamite War of 1689–1691 the Protestant city of <b>Derry</b> withstood a siege of 105 days, William III defeated James II at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690, and the Battle of Aughrim in 1691 preceded the Treaty of Limerick.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1996 Bob Brier suggested that bone fragments in the skull were a sign of a blow to the head, but they were later found to be vertebrae pushed in during <b>Derry</b>'s examination.</blockquote><a href="/work/tomb-of-tutankhamun">Tomb of Tutankhamun · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody, &quot;<b>Londonderry</b> Air&quot;, is a traditional Irish air; Jane Ross of Limavady is credited with collecting it in the mid-19th century from a musician she met.</blockquote><a href="/songs/danny-boy">Danny Boy</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Derry</b>, 13 apprentices shut the town's gates against Jacobite forces, an act of rebellion against James II.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Earhart took off from Harbour Grace, Newfoundland, and landed in a field near <b>Derry</b> the next day.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-20">20 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Bill Clinton addressed crowds in Belfast and <b>Derry</b> in support of the peace process.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-30">30 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
